  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 316 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nThe large upper-level dome of high pressure has led to relentless \nheat and humidity across much of the central US today and is \nexpected to keep heat concerns through Monday. Today, afternoon \ntemperatures in the mid to upper 90s are common as highs are \nexpected to top out a few degrees further in the next few hours. This\npaired with a very oppressive and humid airmass - characterized by \ndewpoints in the upper 70s and low 80s - has led to heat indices in \nthe 105-120 degree range. An Extreme heat Warning has been extended \nto include all of eastern Kansas and portions of central KS. The \nremainder of the area across north-central KS remains under a Heat \nAdvisory.\n\nThe next few days will not see much improvement in the heat as \ntemperatures Sunday and Monday will top out in the upper 90s to low \n100s. Dewpoints Sunday/Monday are expected to mix out a bit under \nslightly windier conditions, but dewpoints still around 70-75 \ndegrees, heat indices will again push into the 105-115 degree range. \nThe first 'cool' day may come by Tuesday as energy rounding the \nupper ridge pushes a weak frontal boundary across the area through \nthe morning and early afternoon. Areas across northern Kansas may \nsee highs top out in the low 90s behind this boundary while areas \nacross east-central and southern Kansas may still warm into the mid \nto upper 90s with heat indices pushing back into the low to mid \n100s. Along with the frontal passage, some low-end PoPs can be \nexpected, mainly across central and north-central KS Tuesday morning \nand afternoon. This could too help to keep things a bit cooler \nduring the day Tuesday and this uncertainty in temperatures Tuesday \nhas kept heat headlines as an Extreme Heat Watch at this time.\n\nA slightly cooler and more seasonable summer pattern sets up \nWednesday and into the weekend as temperatures fall back towards the \nlow 90s. As the upper ridge regresses back west through the week, an \nincrease in mid-level waves will be able to round the ridge and pass \nover the region. This should yield some off an on chances for PoPs \nnear daily with Thursday into Friday looking to be the next best \nchance for widespread precipitation.",
